<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">859–<a href="/years/911">911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Yahya ibn al-Husayn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya ibn al-Husayn was the founder of the Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. A descendant of the Prophet's grandson al-Hasan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Sa'dah</a> in 897 at the invitation of feuding tribes, settled their disputes and set up a state there. His writings founded the Hadawi school of Zaydi law, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> claiming his legacy ruled in Yemen until 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hadi-ila-l-haqq-yahya.jpg" alt="Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown, medieval mint-master. Photo: CNG coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_al-Hadi_ila%27l-Haqq,_AH_298.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
